Securing Grafana with SSO (Single Sign-On)
Lab Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Understand the fundamentals of Single Sign-On (SSO) authentication
Install and configure Grafana on a Linux system
Set up OAuth 2.0 authentication for Grafana using GitHub as an identity provider
Configure Grafana to integrate with external OAuth providers
Test SSO login functionality and verify secure authentication
Troubleshoot common SSO configuration issues
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

Basic understanding of Linux command line operations
Familiarity with web browsers and basic networking concepts
Understanding of authentication and authorization concepts
A GitHub account (free account is sufficient)
Basic knowledge of configuration files and text editing
Lab Environment
Al Nafi provides Linux-based cloud machines for this lab. Simply click Start Lab to access your dedicated Linux machine. The provided machine is bare metal with no pre-installed tools, so you will install all required software during the lab exercises.

Note: All tasks in this lab will be performed on a single Linux machine. No additional virtual machines or remote hosts are required.

Task 1: Environment Setup and Grafana Installation
Subtask 1.1: Update System and Install Dependencies
First, update your system and install the necessary dependencies.

# Update package repositories
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y

# Install required packages
sudo apt install -y wget curl gnupg2 software-properties-common apt-transport-https
Subtask 1.2: Install Grafana
Download and install the latest version of Grafana from the official repository.

# Add Grafana GPG key
wget -q -O - https://packages.grafana.com/gpg.key | sudo apt-key add -

# Add Grafana repository
echo "deb https://packages.grafana.com/oss/deb stable main" | sudo tee -a /etc/apt/sources.list.d/grafana.list

# Update package list and install Grafana
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y grafana

# Enable and start Grafana service
sudo systemctl enable grafana-server
sudo systemctl start grafana-server

# Check service status
sudo systemctl status grafana-server
Subtask 1.3: Verify Grafana Installation
Confirm that Grafana is running properly.

# Check if Grafana is listening on port 3000
sudo netstat -tlnp | grep :3000

# Check Grafana logs
sudo journalctl -u grafana-server -f --no-pager -n 20
Open your web browser and navigate to http://localhost:3000. You should see the Grafana login page. The default credentials are:

Username: admin
Password: admin
Log in and change the default password when prompted.

Task 2: Configure OAuth 2.0 with GitHub
Subtask 2.1: Create GitHub OAuth Application
Before configuring Grafana, you need to create an OAuth application in GitHub.

Log in to your GitHub account
Navigate to Settings > Developer settings > OAuth Apps
Click New OAuth App
Fill in the application details:
Application name: Grafana SSO Lab
Homepage URL: http://localhost:3000
Authorization callback URL: http://localhost:3000/login/github
Click Register application
Note down the Client ID and generate a Client Secret
Important: Keep your Client ID and Client Secret secure. You will need them in the next step.

Subtask 2.2: Configure Grafana for GitHub OAuth
Edit the Grafana configuration file to enable GitHub OAuth authentication.

# Create a backup of the original configuration
sudo cp /etc/grafana/grafana.ini /etc/grafana/grafana.ini.backup

# Edit the Grafana configuration file
sudo nano /etc/grafana/grafana.ini
Locate and modify the following sections in the configuration file:

# Server configuration
[server]
http_port = 3000
domain = localhost
root_url = http://localhost:3000

# Authentication configuration
[auth]
disable_login_form = false
disable_signout_menu = false

# GitHub OAuth configuration
[auth.github]
enabled = true
allow_sign_up = true
client_id = YOUR_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID
client_secret = YOUR_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET
scopes = user:email,read:org
auth_url = https://github.com/login/oauth/authorize
token_url = https://github.com/login/oauth/access_token
api_url = https://api.github.com/user
allowed_organizations = 
team_ids = 
allow_assign_grafana_admin = false
role_attribute_path = 
Replace YOUR_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID and YOUR_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET with the values from your GitHub OAuth application.

Subtask 2.3: Configure User Roles and Permissions
Add additional configuration for user management:

# Users configuration
[users]
allow_sign_up = true
allow_org_create = true
auto_assign_org = true
auto_assign_org_id = 1
auto_assign_org_role = Viewer
verify_email_enabled = false
default_theme = dark

# Security settings
[security]
admin_user = admin
admin_password = 
secret_key = 
disable_gravatar = false
Save the configuration file and exit the editor.

Subtask 2.4: Restart Grafana Service
Apply the configuration changes by restarting Grafana.

# Restart Grafana service
sudo systemctl restart grafana-server

# Check service status
sudo systemctl status grafana-server

# Monitor logs for any errors
sudo journalctl -u grafana-server -f --no-pager -n 30
Task 3: Test SSO Authentication
Subtask 3.1: Test GitHub OAuth Login
Open your web browser and navigate to http://localhost:3000
You should now see a Sign in with GitHub button on the login page
Click the GitHub login button
You will be redirected to GitHub for authentication
Authorize the Grafana application when prompted
You should be redirected back to Grafana and automatically logged in
Subtask 3.2: Verify User Information
Once logged in via GitHub OAuth:

Click on your profile icon in the bottom left corner
Select Profile to view your user information
Verify that your GitHub username and email are displayed correctly
Check the Organizations tab to see your GitHub organizations (if any)
Subtask 3.3: Test Role Assignment
Create a test dashboard to verify permissions:

Navigate to Dashboards > New Dashboard
Add a simple panel with a test query
Save the dashboard with the name "SSO Test Dashboard"
Verify that you can create and modify dashboards based on your assigned role
Subtask 3.4: Test Logout and Re-login
Test the complete authentication flow:

# Check current Grafana sessions
sudo grep -i "login\|logout\|oauth" /var/log/grafana/grafana.log | tail -10
Log out of Grafana using the logout option in the user menu
Attempt to access a protected page directly
Verify that you are redirected to the login page
Log in again using GitHub OAuth
Confirm that the session is properly established
Task 4: Advanced SSO Configuration
Subtask 4.1: Configure Team Mapping
Enhance the OAuth configuration to map GitHub teams to Grafana roles:

# Edit the configuration file again
sudo nano /etc/grafana/grafana.ini
Update the GitHub OAuth section:

[auth.github]
enabled = true
allow_sign_up = true
client_id = YOUR_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID
client_secret = YOUR_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET
scopes = user:email,read:org
auth_url = https://github.com/login/oauth/authorize
token_url = https://github.com/login/oauth/access_token
api_url = https://api.github.com/user
# Map GitHub teams to Grafana roles
team_ids = 
allowed_organizations = YOUR_GITHUB_ORGANIZATION
allow_assign_grafana_admin = true
role_attribute_path = contains(groups[*], '@YOUR_ORG/admin') && 'Admin' || contains(groups[*], '@YOUR_ORG/editor') && 'Editor' || 'Viewer'
Subtask 4.2: Configure Session Management
Add session security settings:

# Session configuration
[session]
provider = file
provider_config = sessions
cookie_name = grafana_sess
cookie_secure = false
session_life_time = 86400
gc_interval_time = 86400
Subtask 4.3: Enable Audit Logging
Configure audit logging to track authentication events:

# Logging configuration
[log]
mode = console file
level = info
filters = 

[log.console]
level = info
format = console

[log.file]
level = info
format = text
log_rotate = true
max_lines = 1000000
max_size_shift = 28
daily_rotate = true
max_days = 7
Restart Grafana to apply the changes:

sudo systemctl restart grafana-server
sudo journalctl -u grafana-server -f --no-pager -n 20
Task 5: Security Verification and Monitoring
Subtask 5.1: Verify Security Headers
Check that proper security headers are configured:

# Test security headers
curl -I http://localhost:3000

# Check for specific security headers
curl -s -D- http://localhost:3000 | grep -i "x-frame-options\|x-content-type-options\|x-xss-protection"
Subtask 5.2: Monitor Authentication Logs
Set up monitoring for authentication events:

# Create a script to monitor OAuth events
cat > ~/monitor_sso.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
echo "Monitoring Grafana SSO authentication events..."
echo "Press Ctrl+C to stop monitoring"
echo "----------------------------------------"

tail -f /var/log/grafana/grafana.log | grep -i --line-buffered "oauth\|github\|login\|logout\|auth"
EOF

chmod +x ~/monitor_sso.sh

# Run the monitoring script
~/monitor_sso.sh
Subtask 5.3: Test Security Scenarios
Perform security testing:

Test with invalid OAuth credentials:

Temporarily modify the client secret in the configuration
Restart Grafana and attempt to log in
Verify that authentication fails gracefully
Test session timeout:

Set a short session timeout in the configuration
Log in and wait for the session to expire
Verify that you are redirected to login
Test unauthorized access:

Log out of Grafana
Try to access http://localhost:3000/api/dashboards/home
Verify that you receive an authentication error
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: OAuth Callback URL Mismatch
Symptoms: Error message about callback URL mismatch during GitHub authentication.

Solution:

# Verify the callback URL in your GitHub OAuth app settings
# It should be: http://localhost:3000/login/github

# Check Grafana configuration
grep -n "root_url" /etc/grafana/grafana.ini
Issue 2: Grafana Service Not Starting
Symptoms: Grafana service fails to start after configuration changes.

Solution:

# Check service status and logs
sudo systemctl status grafana-server
sudo journalctl -u grafana-server --no-pager -n 50

# Validate configuration syntax
sudo grafana-server -config /etc/grafana/grafana.ini -test-config
Issue 3: GitHub OAuth Not Appearing
Symptoms: GitHub login button not visible on the login page.

Solution:

# Verify OAuth configuration
grep -A 10 "\[auth.github\]" /etc/grafana/grafana.ini

# Ensure enabled = true is set
# Restart Grafana service
sudo systemctl restart grafana-server
Issue 4: Permission Denied Errors
Symptoms: Users cannot access certain features after OAuth login.

Solution:

# Check user roles in Grafana logs
sudo grep -i "role\|permission" /var/log/grafana/grafana.log

# Verify role assignment in configuration
grep -A 5 "auto_assign_org_role" /etc/grafana/grafana.ini
Lab Verification
To verify that your SSO configuration is working correctly, perform these final checks:

# 1. Verify Grafana is running with OAuth enabled
curl -s http://localhost:3000/login | grep -i github

# 2. Check configuration syntax
sudo grafana-server -config /etc/grafana/grafana.ini -test-config

# 3. Verify OAuth endpoints are accessible
curl -s "https://github.com/login/oauth/authorize?client_id=YOUR_CLIENT_ID&response_type=code"

# 4. Check authentication logs
sudo tail -20 /var/log/grafana/grafana.log | grep -i oauth
